# 3.13: borrow the best design, and 7th

One lever, measured one switch at a time

Unit 3, talk 8

---

## After 9th: where are the next 2.3 points?

3.12 was 87.72. Four whole-field ideas had come back level. Leigh's plan, "the road from 87.7 to 90", asked the data where 90 was.

The answer was one place: **bullet shielding**.

- Nullstride 2.3.3 shields **287** rumble robots and scores **96.9%** against them live
- Hadur 3.12 scores **84.2%** against the same robots
- Matching it there is worth **+2.99 APS**

On the 172 both had been benched shielding, Hadur 84.9% and Nullstride 97.4%. The opponents are shieldable; the shield falls short.

---

## Bullet shielding in one slide

An enemy's bullet is predictable when it was aimed head-on or with a simple lead. Fire your own bullet so the two paths cross, and it never reaches you.

- Works against simple, deterministic guns, and only those: a list says who
- Every shield bullet costs energy, so a shield that misses loses twice
- Hadur has had a shield mode and a list since the DrussGT route (3.8), and lengthened the list in 3.10

---

## Read the leader's design, then split it into switches

<style scoped>table { font-size: 0.8em; }</style>

The decompiled Nullstride jar showed one design and a longer list. The design differences became five switches in `shieldmode.ShieldDesign`, off by default, so 3.12 stayed 3.12:

| Switch | Nullstride does |
|---|---|
| SHIELD-8 `hold` | never fires at the enemy while shielding |
| SHIELD-9 `learn` | learns from every enemy bullet it meets, not only the ones that hit |
| SHIELD-10 `mode` | aims with the most frequent error, not the mean |
| SHIELD-11 `stay` | no exits inside a round; off for the battle at 150 damage or a silent enemy |
| SHIELD-12 `turn` | adds a base that uses the enemy's body turn |

---

## Step 1: one switch at a time (8 seeds, 89 robots both list)

| Build against 3.12 | Points a robot |
|---|---:|
| hold | **+4.05** (+2.94 to +5.16) |
| + learn, mode | +4.19 |
| + stay | +3.92 |
| + turn | +4.29 |

Each build against the one before it: **-0.05, -0.04, +0.08**. All level.

Holding fire is the whole gain. The cleverer differences measure nothing. 3.13 ships hold, learn, mode and stay (SHIELD-13), the build the list was swept with; turn stays off.

---

## Step 3: the list is the bigger lever

<style scoped>section { font-size: 24px; } table { font-size: 0.85em; }</style>

The step-1 build with shield mode on for everyone, on the **198** robots only Nullstride lists:

- Unpicked: **+6.55 points a robot**, +1.07 APS; 134 of 198 up
- The D5 rule (interval above zero, or open with a mean gain of 9 or more) picked **103**
- On independent seeds the same 103 are worth **+1.11 APS**: not a fluke of the seeds

| Opponent ranks | Robots | Points a robot |
|---|---:|---:|
| 11 to 50 | 8 | **+29.1** |
| 51 to 200 | 22 | +12.0 |
| 201 to 400 | 65 | +8.0 |
| 401 to 700 | 74 | +3.0 |

Phoenix, Hydra, Firebird and Chalk: each +34 to +40.

---

## The gate: the list, then the whole field

| Run | Seeds | Against 3.12 |
|---|---:|---:|
| The 200 listed robots | 8 | **+1.40 APS** (+1.18 to +1.61) |
| The whole 1,212-bot field | 4 | **+1.46 APS** (+1.15 to +1.76) |
| The 1,012 off the list | 4 | -0.006 APS |

Shield mode acts on nobody off the list, and the census agrees. The team's MGUN-6 rode along on its own, team-only path.

Expectation: live kept about 70% of 3.10's shield gain, so about **+1.0**.

---

## Live: 7th of 1,218

**89.50 ± 0.19, 7th**, behind ScalarR (91.23) and Diamond (90.15). +1.78 over 3.12.

| Opponents | Count | Points | APS |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| 3.12's 97 listed | 97 | +4.82 | +0.38 |
| 103 added for 3.13 | 103 | **+14.13** | **+1.20** |
| Unlisted | 1,016 | +0.25 | +0.21 (noise) |

The listed robots gave **+1.58**, more than the gate's +1.40. This time the live pass kept all of it.

---

## The team: same dodging, less firepower

TeamRumble, 3.11: 12th. Measured against HOFSwarm on the same 13 teams:

| | HadurTeam | HOFSwarm |
|---|---:|---:|
| Hits taken per 100 enemy bullets | 17.1 | 16.0 |
| Mean bullet power | **1.35** | **1.93** |
| Mean round length (ticks) | 1,022 | 768 |

Members dodged as well but hit lighter: melee's power table suits ten-bot free-for-alls. MGUN-6: with a living teammate and 30 energy, fire power 3 (+5.7 a team, 45 teams, 6 seeds). Live: **68.30, 9th**. 1v1 and melee never take that path.

---

## What it taught us

- Ask where the points are before choosing a change: one lever held most of the gap to 90
- Copy a leader's design as switches, and bench each one. Most of the cleverness measured nothing
- Who you apply a technique to can matter more than how well you do it: the list gave four times what the design did
- Pick by a rule (D5), then re-measure the picks on fresh seeds to strip the luck of choosing
- A ladder-specific change gets its own code path, so the other ladders cannot move

---

## What is left

- Melee: **17th** at 65.58, unchanged code. The ladder still outside the top 10
- 1v1: the largest shield leak left is `nowave`, enemy shots never detected
- Engine warnings against zen.Mirage are worth a bench look

